course: dictionary
lesson: 11104
title: Which: choosing and linking
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: «Which» is a small word with a big job. Often it asks someone to choose one thing from a group: «Which colour do you want?» You are picking one out of several.
explain: «Which» can also add extra information. «The bike, which is red, is mine» tells you more about the bike, and «He was late, which made me cross» points back to the whole idea that he was late.
explain: To check which job «which» is doing, try swapping in «that» or «the one». If the sentence still makes sense, «which» is describing or linking. In maths, «Which of these two numbers is bigger?» asks you to choose, just like a question about a colour.
fact: «Which» asks for a choice, often a name or a thing, as in «Which one?» or «Which way?».
fact: A comma before «which» often shows extra information, as in «The bike, which is red, is mine».
fact: «Which» can point back to a whole idea, as in «He was late, which made me cross».
fact: In casual talk you may hear «which is why» or «which, fair enough», but these are chatty phrases, not something to copy in a formal essay.
fact: In exams and formal writing you will see «which of the following», «in which», «by which» and «the extent to which».
exercise: In «Which colour do you want?», what job does «which» do? | Tells more about a thing just named | Means «that» in a description | Points back to a whole idea | *Asks someone to choose one thing from a group
exercise: In «He was late, which made me cross», what does «which» point back to? | Nothing, it is a spelling mistake | The word «made» | *The whole idea that he was late | The word «cross»
exercise: Ella is choosing a book and says, «Which one?» What is she asking? | Where the book was bought | How old the book is | Why the book is red | *Which book she should pick
test: «In a flood, never walk in floodwater, which can be fast, dirty and electrified.» What does «which» tell you about the floodwater? | It means the flood is over | It means «witch» | *It tells more about the floodwater | It asks you to choose a flood
test: Exam questions often use «the extent to which». In that phrase, what is «which» doing? | Describing a bike | Naming a colour | *Linking the words to an amount or degree | Asking for a number
exam: Which phrase is the formal exam wording? | which, fair enough | which one? | *the extent to which | which idiot took my pen?
